  | Medium | `/Users/vbb/IdeaProjects/creditsAI/backend/src/main/java/com/creditsai/controller/ClientController.java:63` (with `/Users/vbb/IdeaProjects/creditsAI/backend/src/main/java/com/creditsai/exception/GlobalExceptionHandler.java:77`) | Sorting accepts any property. `?sort=foo` (or `sort=password`) makes the repository throw `PropertyReferenceException`. The catch-all handler turns it into a 500 with the generic message. Nothing leaks to the user, but any caller can trigger server errors and log noise, and it is a client error reported as 500. There is no allow-list of sortable fields. | Add `@ExceptionHandler(PropertyReferenceException.class)` that returns 400 with a user-readable detail ("Sorting by 'x' is not supported."), without echoing the class or entity name. Better: also check `pageable.getSort()` against an allow-list (`name`, `identifier`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `archivedAt`) in the controller or service. |
  | Medium | `/Users/vbb/IdeaProjects/creditsAI/backend/src/main/java/com/creditsai/BackendApplication.java:13`, `/Users/vbb/IdeaProjects/creditsAI/backend/src/main/resources/application.yml` | Page `size` has no explicit cap (rule 5). The only limit is Spring Data's built-in default of 2000, so one call can return 2000 rows. Also, putting `@EnableSpringDataWebSupport` on the app class registers its own `PageableHandlerMethodArgumentResolver`. I believe this makes Boot's `SpringDataWebAutoConfiguration` back off, so any future `spring.data.web.pageable.max-page-size` setting would be silently ignored. I did not verify this against Boot 4.1 sources. | Remove the annotation. In `application.yml`, set `spring.data.web.pageable.serialization-mode: via-dto` and `spring.data.web.pageable.max-page-size: 100` (or another agreed cap). Add a test that `size=100000` is clamped. |
  | Low | `/Users/vbb/IdeaProjects/creditsAI/backend/src/main/java/com/creditsai/controller/ClientController.java:60` | `#archived` depends on `-parameters` name discovery. The `spring-boot-starter-parent` 4.1.2 turns it on, and the custom compiler executions in `pom.xml` do not override it, so it works today. If it ever broke, `!#archived` would evaluate `!null` and SpEL would throw. That fails closed (a 500, not a bypass), but it would break USER listing. | Annotate the parameter with `@P("archived")` (`org.springframework.security.core.parameters.P`) so the expression does not depend on compiler flags. |
  | Low | `/Users/vbb/IdeaProjects/creditsAI/backend/src/test` | There are no tests for the Client resource, so nothing guards the authorization matrix against regressions. The project rule is that every change comes with tests. | Add `@WebMvcTest` and integration tests for: USER `archived=true` gives 403; USER `archived=1` / `archived=on` / `archived=false&archived=true` gives 403; USER with no param gives 200; USER GET of an archived id gives 404; ADMIN GET of an archived id gives 200; USER PUT, DELETE and restore give 403; no token gives 401; a body containing `id`/`archived`/`createdAt` is ignored. |
  
  ### Checks that passed
  
  - **@PreAuthorize on every endpoint**: correct and matches the confirmed design. POST and GET `/{id}` use `hasAnyRole('USER','ADMIN')`. The list uses `hasRole('ADMIN') or (hasRole('USER') and !#archived)`. PUT, DELETE and POST `/{id}/restore` use `hasRole('ADMIN')`. `@EnableMethodSecurity` is on.
  - **Bypassing `#archived`**: I found no bypass. Method security evaluates the argument value after it has been bound, which is the same value the method receives.
    - If the param is omitted, it defaults to `false`, so a USER sees only active clients. This is intended.
    - If it is passed twice, Spring binds the first value, and both SpEL and the service see that same value. There is no mismatch between what is checked and what is used.
    - `yes`, `on` and `1` bind to `true`, so a USER gets 403.
    - A non-boolean value gets 400 (type mismatch from `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler`) before the method runs, and the generic detail has no internals.
  - **Admin detection in GET `/{id}`** (`ClientController.java:77-79`): correct. It compares against `ROLE_ADMIN`, exactly what `KeycloakRealmRoleConverter` produces. `authentication` cannot be null because `anyRequest().authenticated()` comes first. A USER gets the same 404 message for archived and missing ids, so there is no enumeration oracle.
  - **Mass assignment**: not possible. `ClientRequest` has only name, identifier, address and description. `Client` has setters only for those four fields, so MapStruct cannot set id, archived, archivedAt or the timestamps. `ReportingPolicy.ERROR` plus the missing setters keeps it that way. Extra JSON properties are ignored.
  - **Input lengths**: fine. The `@Size` values (100/50/200/500) match the `VARCHAR` columns in `001-create-clients.sql`, `@NotBlank` matches `NOT NULL`, and the controller uses `@Valid` on POST and PUT. Java counts UTF-16 units, which is stricter than Postgres's character count, so nothing overflows. One small edge: a `\u0000` character makes Postgres reject the insert, which ends up as a generic 500. That leaks nothing.
  - **Queries**: fine. They are derived queries only (`findByArchived`, `findByIdAndArchivedFalse`), with no JPQL strings and no native SQL. Nothing is physically deleted: archive and restore are entity methods.
  - **Errors**: fine. `ProblemDetail` everywhere, the catch-all logs and returns a generic 500, AccessDenied gives 403, and `server.error.include-stacktrace/message: never`. The not-found and business messages are readable by users.
  - **Token validation, filter chain, CORS, actuator and secrets**: all fine.
    - The issuer and JWKS URIs are both configured, and an `azp` validator is in place.
    - Realm roles map to `ROLE_*` in a single converter.
    - The chain is stateless with CSRF off, and default-deny with only `/actuator/health` permitted.
    - CORS has no allowed origins, not `*`.
    - Actuator exposes `health` only, and the DB password comes from `${CREDITS_DB_PASSWORD}`.
  
